<!--
What the clubs actually do: a ball, a stage mask, music.

  The brief was a reference illustration of the "extracurricular activities"
  kind — basketball, theatre masks, notes scattered around them — without its
  caption. This is that composition in the app's own material: white line work
  at the two weights used everywhere else, no fills in palette colours. The
  reference is flat colour on white; the same drawing filled in orange and
  yellow over a dark green mesh reads as clip-art pasted onto glass, which is
  the mistake the first version of these tiles made and the reason the note
  about it below has survived four rewrites.

  Two weights are load-bearing here rather than decorative. The ball outline,
  the mask and the near note are the drawing; the seams, the face and the far
  note are at 54% so the cluster has a front and a back. Everything at full
  weight and four objects in one tile flattens into a tangle.

  The nav bar's `ic_booths.xml` is NOT this drawing — it is the mask alone. Four
  objects with interior detail is a smudge at 22dp; that is not a guess, it was
  rendered at true size and looked at. The mask is the piece that survives, so
  the two share a subject and a hand rather than an outline. Change the style
  here and the mask there has to follow.

  It replaces a staggered wall of tiles, and before that a market stall. Both
  drew the *screen* rather than the thing — accurate, and nothing a student
  wants to look at on a tile that says "Clubs".

  The accent stays off it. Lime is the app's action colour: a scanned
  checkpoint, a filled progress bar, things that mean something has happened. A
  drawing on a tile has not happened, and three lime drawings on one screen made
  a page of white type look decorated rather than laid out.
-->
